WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.760
An engineering workstation contains specialized software used to configure, program, diagnose, and maintain control-system components.

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:15.989
It may hold controller projects, HMI applications, device profiles, firmware tools, network settings, and vendor utilities.

00:00:15.989 --> 00:00:25.160
Because it can make privileged operational changes, it is one of the most security-sensitive assets in an ICS.

00:00:25.160 --> 00:00:30.098
Engineering work should follow approved pathways and change procedures.

00:00:30.098 --> 00:00:38.327
Dedicated accounts, controlled removable media, verified software, protected project repositories, and known-good backups help preserve integrity.

00:00:38.327 --> 00:00:45.617
Remote engineering access should be time-limited, monitored, and tied to a specific work authorization.

00:00:45.617 --> 00:00:49.144
A historian performs a different role.

00:00:49.144 --> 00:00:55.022
It collects process values, alarms, events, and production information over time.

00:00:55.022 --> 00:01:06.544
Operators and engineers use trends for troubleshooting and performance analysis, while business systems may consume selected data for reporting, planning, or quality functions.

00:01:06.544 --> 00:01:12.422
Historians are high-value because their data reveals how the process behaves.

00:01:12.422 --> 00:01:17.360
They also often connect operational and enterprise information flows.

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:23.709
Architecture should avoid uncontrolled direct access from business networks into control systems.

00:01:23.709 --> 00:01:30.528
Replication, brokered interfaces, or services in an industrial DMZ can reduce unnecessary paths.

00:01:30.528 --> 00:01:38.758
Access control should separate users who view data from users who administer collection, interfaces, or retention.

00:01:38.758 --> 00:01:45.577
Monitor changes to tags, collection sources, replication destinations, service identities, and time settings.

00:01:45.577 --> 00:01:51.925
Protect historian availability and integrity so incident responders can trust the timeline.

00:01:51.925 --> 00:01:57.804
Engineering workstations can change the process; historians can explain the process.

00:01:57.804 --> 00:02:03.682
Both deserve strong inventory, endpoint management, backup, logging, and recovery planning.

00:02:03.682 --> 00:02:11.442
Security teams should preserve their specialized functions rather than treating them as interchangeable office computers.
